  "query_preview": "/Through Wednesday/...\nIssued at 253 PM MDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nCurrent analysis shows the moist airmass from AZ/NM has infiltrated \nour area. PWATs sit at 0.8-0.9 in the foothills and Denver metro. \nTowards the eastern plains they increase to 1.0-1.3. Current radar \ndepicts showers developing in the plains where some peaks of \nsunshine still remain. The next 36 hours or so of the forecast \nremain highly uncertain in regards to placement and development of \nconvective activity. \n\nModel guidance displays a wide range of solutions, and combined with \nthe cloud shield across the forecast area we are concerned this will \nlimit instability significantly thus lowering the chances for \nshower development. Current ACARS DEN sounding also indicates we \nare capped, but with abundant moisture aloft. Furthermore, the \nshortwave that was advertised to move through has shifted to far \nSoutheastern Colorado towards the Kansas border limiting \nwidespread chances for our forecast area. Even with the lack of \ntrue lift, the amount of moisture present should be enough to \nproduce showers for most of the area. Here is a breakdown of the \nchances. The eastern and southern plains will see the greatest \nrainfall from this system ~60-70% through around Thursday \nafternoon, and the urban corridor and Denver metro will see about \na 40-60% chance. South Parks chances will increase to ~60-80% \nthrough Thursday evening. Some model guidance depicts drier air \nfiltering in from the northwest so the Northern Mountains could \nsee more limited activity, especially tonight. \n\nThursday morning model guidance has convection shifting off to the \nsouth and east of our area, but as the afternoon approaches we \nshould see another increase in shower activity in the higher terrain \naround noon (~60%), and urban corridor/Denver Metro later in the \nafternoon (~40-50%). Some CAMs depict a round of scattered to \nwidespread storms moving pushing through the urban corridor to \neastern plains in the evening while short term guidance like the \nNAMNEST and HRRR isn't exactly on board with this. It is also \nworth it to note storm motion for today and Thursday is between \n5-15kts and the 12,18Z runs of the HRRR indicate a storm that \ntrains over the Cameron Peak burn scar area for a few hours. This \nis only one solution, but the potential for slow moving storms \nwith localized flash flooding will be possible through Thursday \nevening. If enough cloud cover clears Thursday night we could see\nthe potential for patchy fog to develop mostly in the plains and \nmountain valleys. \n\nFriday most model guidance depicts a shortwave move into our \nforecast area bringing shower/storm potential. PWATs are still \nexpected to be 170-190% above normal and forecast soundings indicate \nCAPE will increase with limited CIN. The GFS is an outlier currently \nand has the precipitation occurring further south of our area. \nTemperatures will warm back up to mid/upper 70s. \n\nSaturday and Sunday still trends drier and warmer up to the low/mid \n80s in the urban corridor with 60s in the higher terrain. Monday and \nTuesday temperatures cool down due to the passage of an upper \nlevel trough potentially bringing our next best chance for \nprecipitation.",
